            <p>July AAPL trades <strong>21.65¢</strong> with 5 trading days left; August trades <strong>25¢</strong> with 26. The kink partially closed after Sunday's buying (the AM run caught it at 0.4¢ of spread; it is now 3.35¢) — but August is still ~11.5 pts below what July's own implied vol prices it at: the σ that reproduces July's 21.65¢ (<strong>1.32%/day</strong>) puts August at ≈<strong>36.5¢</strong> and December at ≈<strong>43.4¢</strong>. The implied-vol term structure still collapses after Friday (Jul 1.32%/d → Aug 0.67 → Dec 0.38) while realized ran ~4.5% relative in one session on Jul 24. Expressions: long AAPL-Aug YES outright (trade #1), or the calendar spread (buy Aug / sell Jul) — near-flat on a flip this week, pure optionality if not.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="panel-card">
            <h3 class="panel-title"><span class="pm-badge pm-rich">2 · VOL INVERSION</span> Alphabet priced ~5× more volatile than Apple — now in two months</h3>
            <p>December: AAPL-Dec 28.1¢ implies σ<sub>rel</sub> ≈ <strong>0.38%/day</strong>; GOOGL-Dec 9.5¢ implies ≈ <strong>1.82%/day</strong> — a 4.8× ratio on pairs sharing the same NVDA leg. Not defensible. And the August books now show the same distortion: GOOGL-2nd-Aug trades 11–13¢ against a cap-math fair of ~1.3%. Expressions: the executed December pair (long AAPL-Dec YES ✓ / GOOGL-Dec NO ✓ — complete the long half), and the new GOOGL-3rd-Aug YES at 90¢ (trade #5), which is short the same overpriced tail.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="panel-card">
            <h3 class="panel-title"><span class="pm-badge pm-watch">3 · MOMENTUM PREMIUM</span> NVDA priced with heavy drift</h3>
            <p>Zero-drift fair for NVDA is ~69 / 57 / 39 (Jul / Aug / Dec) vs market <strong>80 / 72 / 57.5</strong> — the premium widened overnight as July NVDA ticked up to 79.5¢. To justify these prices NVDA must out-grow Apple by roughly 1–2% per month, every month, through December (at σ=2%/d). That remains the market's actual bet. If you believe in AI-capex momentum, the book is fine and the "cheap" flags shrink; if you believe big-cap relative performance is near a random walk, NVDA NO is systematically overpriced across all three dates. Decide which world you're in before trading anything here.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="panel-card">
            <h3 class="panel-title"><span class="pm-badge pm-fair">4 · NO FREE LUNCH</span> The books themselves stay tight — December now sums under 98</h3>
            <p>Sum of YES mids: July <strong>101.55¢</strong>, August <strong>102.85¢</strong>, December <strong>97.95¢</strong>. Buying every listed December YES at mids costs ~96.5¢ for a basket that pays $1 if the winner is listed — ~+3.6% over five months at mids, but crossing real asks pushes the cost to ≈98¢ and the July/August books price at a premium, not a discount. The overrounds moved overnight (July was 100.4 at the AM run) without creating a Dutch book. The edge on this page still lives in <em>relative</em> pricing (cards 1–3), not in the sums.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
